From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0250] feat(rsc): one apps executor per boot, chosen from the device tree

C3-04 is an ownership change and nothing else: for a boot whose tree asks for
it, the RSC channel becomes the single executor of the apps array - it owns the
TCS window, the line, the interrupt and every store - while the display service
keeps its clients, its gate, its order, its deadlines and its failure retention
and simply has no way to touch the hardware any more.  There is no runtime
handover and no draining of in-flight state: the mode is decided at start from
the tree, and the other owner's entry points are already closed by the time
either side publishes anything.

The door.  SweetAppsDoor.h/.cpp is the one place where the two sides meet: a
small interface (read, write, identity, triggerReady, arm, unmask, quiet, stop,
irq, install, store/irq counters) plus a publish/withdraw pair over a single
pointer.  Publish is a compare-and-exchange under a lazily allocated simple lock
and refuses a second publisher; withdraw only accepts the pointer that is
actually held, so a stale owner cannot dismantle its successor.  The lock is
malloc'd, never a lock group, because this code runs before either service is
ready and the first caller may be an interrupt.  A boot that never takes the
line publishes no table at all - the table is published where the line is
claimed, in the owner's IRQ setup, not in start() - because a table nobody can
call into would advertise operations that do not exist.

The line.  SweetAppsLine.h/.cpp holds the GIC-side sequence both owners use:
geometry and claimable checks that only read, program/retired for the
redistributor's asynchronous phase, verify() that samples the whole state before
forming a verdict, mask/unmask, and admit().  It is deliberately a second,
separate implementation from the older inline sequence inside SweetRPMh: that
sequence has five device rings of evidence behind it and is frozen, and
unifying it here would change a path this card does not touch.

The channel as executor.  With sweet,rsc-executor the node names a fourth range
(the GIC window, 0x17a00000/0x10000) and IOQcomRSC maps it, validates the
controller geometry, checks that the line is not already live, creates its own
work loop and one interrupt event source, then publishes the door.  Its top half
masks the line, counts the entry, and posts the event source; the client's
completion runs on the work loop thread with no channel lock held, so the
completion is free to come straight back through the door and program the next
flight.  Stores go through tcsWrite, which refuses outside the window and
outside executor mode, counts every accepted store, and keeps the ordering rules
the channel already had.  The line is unmasked only after a trigger has been
admitted, and quieted on stop before the event source is removed.  The two rows
that describe the line and the TCS words say which role they describe, because
the same binary prints both depending on the tree: on a delegating boot they read
described-for-executor and observed-before-owner, and on every other tree the
older not-armed and not-owned wording is unchanged, so a ring still answers the
question 'who had the hardware' on its own.

The display service as client.  With sweet,rpmh-executor-delegated it maps only
the locator and the command DB (read-only, kIODirectionIn), takes no TCS, DRV or
GIC window, registers no interrupt handler, arms no timer-driven poll, and sends
every store through the door.  Its identity now comes from the channel's door
rather than from registers it does not map: adoptDelegatedIdentity asks once and
keeps the answer.  installDelegated binds the completion callback, and the
completion arrives on the channel's work loop.  ensureExecutor() gates all three
flight entry points (rail/panel vote, display corner, register bandwidth) on
"a door exists, an identity is known, a completion is bound"; without any of
those the request is refused with a row and the service does not fall back to
its own mapping, because that fallback is exactly the second MMIO path this card
removes.  If the door disappears mid-flight the store path refuses and poisons
the service instead of writing around the table.

Why no boot argument.  The boot-argument budget is full (607 bytes, 605 in use)
and this is a topology decision, not a tunable, so the two properties are the
only switch.  Rollback is therefore a property edit on an image that is
otherwise the same, which is also why the old owner's ranges and INTID are left
untouched by the generator: only its role changes.

The generator.  --rsc-executor requires --rsc-channel and --rpmh-irq and refuses
to emit a one-sided tree: a channel delegated to by a node that does not stand
down, or a stand-down with no channel, would boot with no writer at all.  The
derived tree differs from the C3-03 tree by one property per node and by the
channel's fourth range, so removing the two properties and the range restores
the earlier bytes exactly; the generator test asserts that restoration.

Tests.  test-mh2lm-rsc-channel.py gains the executor cases: a start that admits
the line, a line that is already live, and a second executor whose door publish
is refused.  test-mh2lm-rsc-executor-door.py is new and drives the two modes
against the same fixtures: the module-mode boot and the delegated boot must
produce the same TCS store trajectory from the same vote, the delegated boot must
map only locator+DB (two maps, direction In), must not map the array or arm a
line, must adopt the owner's identity, must count zero self stores, and must
refuse - with a row, a missing token and no store - once the door is withdrawn.
The ADT generator test gains the C3-04 composition and both refusals.  Nothing
in sleh.c changes: sweet_rpmh_irq() already routes INTID37 to whichever owner
published, and the chain it sits in is untouched.

The status word the admission step needs.  admit() takes a thunk that reads the
TCS status register, and that read is a member: the class keeps the array to
itself, so the thunk is a static member of the channel, declared beside the
door adapters that exist for the same reason.  A file-scope helper cannot reach
tcsRead() - the first compiler to see the whole unit said so, and that is the
only thing the CI build of card C3-04 found - and the alternative, widening the
member, would hand every caller in this translation unit the array this card is
careful to keep private.  The host fixtures had been blind to this because they
compile copies whose 'class' was rewritten to 'struct' so the cases can poke
members; both fixtures that compile this unit now also compile the shipped text
once, unchanged, and assert that a file-scope helper is still refused.

